ent/pandan"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"],["Cinnamon","ingredient/cinnamon"],["Palm sugar","ingredient/palm-sugar"],["Black adzuki bean","ingredient/black-adzuki-bean"],["Glutinous rice","ingredient/glutinous-rice"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/8/86/Mung_beans_%28Vigna_radiata%29.jpg/500px-Mung_beans_%28Vigna_radiata%29.jpg","w":500,"h":333,"by":"Ivar Leidus","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mung_beans_(Vigna_radiata).jpg"}},"ingredient/egg-yolks":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/egg-yolks","n":"Egg yolks","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 3 dishes across 3 cuisines","intro":[["Among animals which produce eggs, the yolk is the nutrient-bearing portion of the egg, the primary function of which is to supply food for the development of the embryo.  Some types of egg contain no yolk, for example because they are laid in situations where the food supply is sufficient (such as in the body of the host of a parasitoid) or because the embryo develops in the parent's body, which supplies the food, usually through a placenta.  Reproductive systems in which the mother's body supplies the embryo directly are said to be matrotrophic; those in which the embryo is supplied by yolk are said to be lecithotrophic.  In many species, such as all birds, and most reptiles and insects, the yolk takes the form of a special storage organ constructed in the reproductive tract of the mother.  In many other animals, especially very small species such as some"]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-egg-yolks"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Egg_yolks"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":3,"groups":[["Portuguese cuisine","cuisine/portuguese-cuisine",[["Bolo de bolacha","dish/bolo-de-bolacha"],["Encharcada","dish/encharcada"],["Tocino de cielo","dish/tocino-de-cielo"]]],["Anda